OpenWings patch 0018: several browser actions in one call (batch), and hover

OpenManus's browser runs Python the model writes against the browser, so
one step can walk twenty pages of a table or open every invoice in a list.
OpenWings' browser did one action per call: a "download the last 24 monthly
statements" task took one model round per click and ran out of rounds.
OpenWings does not run model written code against the signed in browser.
Instead it adds a declarative batch of its own actions, each checked
exactly as if it were called alone (wings/browser.py).

  * src/browser_identity.py: "batch" and "hover" are page actions. A batch
    takes steps (up to 20 of open, read, find, click, fill, press, scroll,
    wait, back, forward, drag and hover), each (repeat the steps for up to
    50 items, with {item} replaced) and pages (repeat them on up to 25
    pages, clicking the next control in between). wings/browser.py checks
    the steps itself: no uploads, screenshots, tabs or JavaScript inside a
    batch, at most 100 step runs and 5 minutes.
  * hover: moves the pointer over a ref, the element showing some text, or
    a screen point, so menus and tooltips that open only on hover appear in
    the outline (OpenManus's browser can hover; OpenWings could not). Hovering
    commits nothing, and clicks on what it reveals are guarded as usual.
  * src/tool_schemas.py: private_browser describes batch and hover.
  * src/agent_loop.py: upstream's checks on the addresses a browser batch
    opens (no example.com placeholders, off topic opens become a search)
    read OpenWings' "steps" as well as upstream's "commands".

The request authority grants batch and hover only together with the other
interactive browser actions (a browser task the person asked for). A step
that needs the person (a password, a code, a card, a pay or send button)
stops the batch with the usual PAUSED message, listing what was done.

